Clean up after a failed volume attach or detach

A volume attach creates its Cinder attachment in the conductor
(`ComputeTaskManager.attach_volume`, nova/conductor/manager.py), which
reserves the volume, and casts the attach to the compute service. When the
attach fails there, `ComputeManager._attach_volume`
(nova/compute/manager.py) deletes the attachment and then destroys the block
device mapping. `nova.volume.cinder.API.attachment_delete` retries a 500 or
504 five times without a wait and a connection failure not at all. When
Cinder is without its database for longer, the attachment stays and the
mapping goes: no detach and no server delete finds an attachment that no
mapping refers to, and the volume stays reserved or attaching, which Cinder
refuses to delete. The conductor's own rollback destroys the mapping
without deleting the attachment when recording the instance action or the
cast to the compute service fails after Cinder created it.

A detach has the opposite gap. The API sets the volume to detaching and
casts the detach. When the compute service's first call to Cinder fails,
`ComputeManager.detach_volume` gives up and nothing rolls the status back:
`roll_detaching` runs only when the hypervisor refuses the detach. Cinder
refuses a detach of a detaching volume, so the volume stays attached and
detaching until its server is deleted.

Nightly chaos found the attach gap on the random leg (issues #1599, #1600
and #1602, seed 41758059024): `cinder-mariadb-partition` cut the Cinder API
from its database for 120 seconds while an attach was in flight. Nova
answered the attach, the volume never got in-use, every later detach
answered 404, and dizzy's teardown failed on the delete with a 400 and
exited 1. The stuck-resources Job found the volume reserved. A local
replay of that fault under attach and detach load reproduced both gaps on
the published image: "Delete attachment failed" after a failed attach left
a volume attaching with no mapping, and detaches that failed on their
first Cinder call left their volumes detaching: 7 of 8 volumes after
eleven 120-second partitions. With the patch the same load left all 8
available or in-use.

Both attach rollbacks now delete the attachment, and a failed detach rolls
the detaching status back, through `compute_utils.call_cinder_with_retries`.
It retries a 5xx or a connection failure with waits that add up to 254
seconds, the bound of cinder patch 0007, which outlasts a database
failover. The conductor deletes the attachment before it destroys the
mapping. A 404 of the delete is ignored as before; `roll_detaching` leaves
a volume that is no longer detaching alone, so a detach that got further
before it failed is not undone. The compute service holds the instance lock
during the retries, so another operation on the same server waits for
them: a slow rollback during an outage is the price of a volume that stays
usable. The attach to a shelved offloaded server in the API has the same
shape as the conductor's and is left alone, because an API worker should
not wait minutes.

Tests: test_attach_volume_raises_new_flow,
test_attach_volume_ignore_VolumeAttachmentNotFound,
test_attach_volume_attachment_create_fails and
test_detach_volume_api_raises pass unchanged. The patch adds
CallCinderWithRetriesTestCase (nova/tests/unit/compute/test_utils.py),
test_attach_volume_raises_new_flow_retries_attachment_delete,
test_detach_volume_rolls_back_detaching and
test_attach_volume_cast_fails_deletes_attachment; the last three fail
without the patch.

Proof: tests/container-images/verify_nova.sh asserts in the built image that
both attach rollbacks delete the attachment, the compute's after a failed
first delete, and that a failed detach rolls the volume back.

Applies to nova 33.0.0 (2026.1).

Upstream status: not yet proposed.

